1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:12,030
Your tests pass. Your users still get stuck. Usabilify gives you a panel of AI beta testers, each one a different kind of person, who try your app on every build.

2
00:00:12,481 --> 00:00:18,311
This is Dana. She is red-green color blind, and she wants to turn off a weekly email.

3
00:00:18,763 --> 00:00:28,083
On the left is the real screen. On the right is what Dana sees. We don't tell the model to act color blind. We take the red away before it looks.

4
00:00:28,534 --> 00:00:31,014
Here is Dana, in her own words.

5
00:00:54,469 --> 00:01:04,169
The setting did change. An end-to-end test for this toggle passes. Dana never knew it worked. That is a green build, and a user who doesn't trust it.

6
00:01:04,619 --> 00:01:14,339
Priya is on her phone, on a bus. Her taps land with thumb-sized error, so a settings gear twenty pixels wide gets missed for real, and she has to notice.

7
00:01:14,791 --> 00:01:18,441
She tries it three times. Then she does what people do.

8
00:01:34,984 --> 00:01:46,604
Walt is seventy-one. He looks back at the screen two seconds after he acts. The confirmation lasts a second and a half, so it is gone before he sees it. The test saw it. Walt didn't.

9
00:01:47,054 --> 00:02:00,654
Kim can't use a mouse. Ray hears the page one line at a time. The menu that holds the team page is a div with a click handler, so to Ray it is just the letters J D, and neither of them can reach it at all.

10
00:02:01,104 --> 00:02:13,535
Every beta tester is built, not prompted. Each one is a model of a person with parts you can open: what she sees, where her clicks land, what she already knows, and how long she will put up with you.

11
00:02:13,986 --> 00:02:23,436
Run the whole panel on every build. Seven people, three tasks, three minutes, under two dollars. The grid shows which kind of person failed where.

12
00:02:23,887 --> 00:02:27,307
Usabilify. Beta testers, engineered.
